WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.230 --> 00:06.880
So the first step in the hypothesis-led approach is to define the problem.
And so when you're beginning to define the problem —

2
00:07.240 --> 00:09.600
what is it that makes a good problem question?

3
00:09.860 --> 00:14.790
One way to do this is to think: is it a SMART question?

4
00:15.170 --> 00:21.060
Is it specific? Is it measurable?
Is it actionable? Is it relevant? And is it time-bound?

5
00:21.610 --> 00:25.890
And so if we think about different problem questions —
if you think about the first

6
00:26.340 --> 00:31.460
test, is it specific? You want to think about:
what is the scope of it? Is it sufficiently narrow?

7
00:31.910 --> 00:37.200
Are we trying to improve the value of the company?
Or, more specifically, are we trying to grow revenue?

8
00:37.200 --> 00:43.940
Measurable. This is around thinking about how we're going to quantify, or think about
the key performance indicators, to know if we've achieved our goal.

9
00:44.330 --> 00:48.400
And so if you're growing revenue,
are you growing it by 10% or by 30%?

10
00:48.400 --> 00:51.110
So, actionable. Is it something you can actually do?

11
00:51.590 --> 00:54.950
If you say, “We want to enter a new market
and capture 10% market share” —

12
00:55.430 --> 01:00.420
how are you going to do that? More specifically, what are the actions
you need to take in order to make that true?

13
01:00.810 --> 01:06.200
So, relevant. Is your problem relevant
for all the stakeholders involved,

14
01:06.200 --> 01:11.330
and have you aligned everybody towards those goals? So, time-bound:
what is the time horizon in which the problem is solved?

15
01:11.910 --> 01:19.750
If your business is facing bankruptcy, you may only have four weeks of cash left.
In those cases, the solution set is going to be a lot different.

16
01:20.200 --> 01:26.600
In other cases, you may be thinking about infrastructure over a 20-to-30-year horizon.
That's again going to lead to much different approaches to the problem.

17
01:26.600 --> 01:34.690
So let's think about an example of how we can use the SMART tool to refine a problem statement —
and I'll give you an example from my life, to keep it simple.

18
01:35.080 --> 01:39.200
So the problem statement could be around:
where do we want to go on vacation?

19
01:39.590 --> 01:46.400
More specifically, where do my wife and I want to go on vacation? Now, that's a pretty
vague definition of the problem. So how can we improve it?

20
01:46.760 --> 01:48.070
Well, let's use the SMART tool.

21
01:48.450 --> 01:56.260
Is it specific enough? Well, have I thought about who's going on vacation? Am I going on vacation?
Is my wife going on vacation? Hopefully the two of us are going on vacation.

22
01:56.870 --> 01:59.550
Is it measurable? Well, what do I want to achieve?

23
01:59.880 --> 02:05.600
Do we want to have a fun time on our vacation, or do we want to make it
the best vacation ever? Maybe we want to make it the best vacation ever.

24
02:05.960 --> 02:08.990
It's a qualitative assessment, but it's still measurable. Actionable?

25
02:09.410 --> 02:16.550
Hopefully in this case it is — we're actually going to go on vacation together.
And relevant? Well, certainly for me and for my wife, it should be relevant for us.

26
02:16.970 --> 02:20.640
And time-bound: specifically, when do we want to go on vacation?

27
02:21.030 --> 02:27.800
Well, we may rephrase it to say: where do we want to go on vacation
to have the best vacation ever within the next four months?

28
02:27.800 --> 02:33.830
And that'll then define the scope of the solution space for us.
So why is having a SMART problem statement up front so important?

29
02:34.530 --> 02:42.150
It's so important because, by ensuring that your problem is SMART-ly defined,
it enables you to get to the right answer more quickly.

30
02:42.500 --> 02:49.570
And so the other day I was working on a problem around:
how do we grow our revenue by a certain amount next year?

31
02:49.990 --> 02:56.200
Now, initially we thought about a number of different ways we could do this.
We could hire more people and increase the size of our sales force.

32
02:56.200 --> 03:01.600
We could spend more on marketing and promotions.
We could think about what was our pricing strategy, et cetera.

33
03:01.600 --> 03:07.320
But as we thought about it more deeply:
what were the constraints that we were actually facing?

34
03:07.490 --> 03:15.400
We realized budgets were fixed, and so spending more on marketing or hiring
more people into our sales force actually was not part of the solution set.

35
03:15.400 --> 03:22.200
And so when we redefined the problem statement to say:
given the existing budget that we have,

36
03:22.530 --> 03:27.600
then how do we increase our revenue by a certain amount in this coming year —
that enabled us to be much more efficient

37
03:27.600 --> 03:32.770
and prioritize more effectively where we're spending our time,
and get to the right answer sooner.
